<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<FindBugsFilter>
    <!--
      Tolerant baseline for SpotBugs.

      Only accepted false positives / by-design patterns live here, each with a reason.
      Do NOT use this file to hide real bugs: fix those in code.
      Prefer narrowing by class/method over excluding a whole bug pattern.
    -->

    <!--
      Entities, DTOs and @ConfigurationProperties expose and accept mutable references through
      manual getters/setters/builder (project style: NO Lombok). This is by design for the
      persistence and transport layers, so the "may expose internal representation" family is
      not actionable here. Revisit if value objects are introduced.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
    </Match>
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      A one-shot SecureRandom is intentionally created per call to generate a temporary secret.
      Not a bug for this use case; revisit if a shared RNG is introduced.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="com.lifecontrol.api.usersadmin.service.UsersAdminService"/>
        <Method name="createUser"/>
        <Bug pattern="DMI_RANDOM_USED_ONLY_ONCE"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      `FieldViolationException` is the abstract base of the four catalog refusals, and its constructor
      refuses a blank field name — the guard that makes "only a refusal that genuinely names a field
      produces an errors map" true by construction rather than by convention.

      SpotBugs reads a throwing constructor as the finalizer-attack pattern, which is real for an
      extendable class whose instances can be left partially initialized and reachable by a subclass
      finalizer. It does not apply here: the class is abstract, so no instance of it is ever created on
      its own; its four subclasses are final in fact (none declares a finalizer, and the codebase
      declares none anywhere), and the exception holds two strings and no resource. The alternative, moving the guard into the handler, would trade a construction-time refusal for a silently
      unmapped refusal, which is the failure this exception exists to prevent.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="com.lifecontrol.api.exception.FieldViolationException"/>
        <Bug pattern="CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW"/>
    </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
